  "notes": "A British tropical-house producer whose model is \"the downtempo four-on-the-floor remake of a known song\", and the entry gives more hard numbers than any other tropical entry here. The tempo is 110-120 \"with a documented 114 on the breakthrough\", so the mode is 114 — the slow end of the lane. The key is documented too: \"bright majors — the breakthrough sits in A major — with a diatonic four-chord loop (I-V-vi-IV family) and no modal borrowing… the most consonant harmonic profile in the section\", which is why the scale set is major and major-pentatonic only, with no minor and no borrowed degree anywhere in the families. And the vocal casting is documented as a range: \"toplines are cast for a mid-range, unforced voice (the breakthrough's lead sits inside roughly E3-F♯4)\", encoded literally as a melody register of 52-72 — the lowest lead register in this section, and the one number here that came from the research rather than from a reading. The kit is \"a soft four-square kick, clap or finger-snap on 2&4, offbeat open hat, and a steel-pan/shaker/bongo 16th bed; fills are percussion crescendos rather than snare rolls, and there is no white-noise riser — the lift is done by adding layers\", so the ladder is off, there is no riser lane at all, and the perc bed runs 5-8 hits a bar. His stated aim is \"a very kind of Swedish-y kind of sound… that's the influence behind that kind of lead synth line\" — a clean, mid-register plucked lead. ⚠ The percussion detail is tropical-house convention by the entry's own statement; the velocities and densities are INFERRED.",
